      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
During the blower door test, there were many areas that had associated air leakage from your home:

** The 6" chimney for the current oil furnace.

** Many of the windows do not close fully anymore.

** The header above the basement wall that adjoins the neighbouring unit

** The wall plugs &amp; switches in exterior walls, and the attic access panel

** The ceiling in the renovated bathroom where the wood ceiling &amp; exterior walls meet

Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I estimate you can achieve about a 20% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 276 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.9 GJ, and qualify for a $7500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>
The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 R12. We discussed your interest to possibly add more insulation to the exterior of these walls in conjunction with the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For this house, I have modeled adding R20, bringing the total R-value up to about R32, just slightly higher than the minimum R30 that is generally required for new construction in the NWT.

Savings:
By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R20, you could potentially save $ 566 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 18.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP),This rebate is for increasing 100% of the exterior walls, including the wall between the main floor that adjoins the rear mud room.

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>
The thermo panes within the windows of this house (all except for the basement) were last updated in 1982 (37 years ago) and were good windows at the time. Some of the hardware is failing &amp; some do not close well at all. If you proceed with an exterior wall insulation upgrade of a minimum additional R10, this would represent a good time to update the windows, as you could qualify for the new $250 per window rebate.

I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of the current windows with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.

Savings: 
By replacing the current windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 396 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12.8 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window (up to 12 window) in rebates through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 

To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program
</windows>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>
Your house currently has a 189 litre electric hot water tank. For you future planning, we talked about an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.

Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 682 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house is currently heated with an older oil furnace (estimated 83% efficiency), and you have a wood stove. For the basis of this energy evaluation, NRCan has us complete the analyses based upon the primary heating system for the house - which is your oil furnace. I have performed savings calculations within the "Additional Comments" section of this report to estimate the savings (net of fuel savings and a basic self-harvested wood rate of $100/cord) associated with 75% of your annual heating being supplied by the wood stove.

In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade from the oil furnace to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. The high efficiency motor will be great for distributing the heat from your woodstove throughout the house.

Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 577 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 27.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose not to participate in the DHERP, you would still be eligible for a $1,500 rebate on this furnace through our Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP)

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

For more information and rebate application forms for EEIP, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>
Wood Stove Supplemental Heat

I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of propane saved from a new high efficiency furnace and a basic self-harvested wood rate of $100/cord) if you were to use the wood stove for about 75% of the heating season and use the high efficiency blower motor of the new furnace to circulate the wood heat throughout the house.

Savings: By using your wood stove for 75% of the heating season, you could potentially save $ 2,002 per year in energy costs, in addition to the energy savings previously outlined for installing the 95% efficient propane furnace.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:
        - no kitchen range hood fan
        - one bathroom fan
        - a clothes dryer that exhausts to the exterior

In addition, there is a 5" fresh air supply that connects to the return air ductwork of the furnace. As discussed, it is not a good setup to have the fresh air intake right next to the clothes dryer exhaust !!

You may want to consider installing a timer switch connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so the fan can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the bathroom fan and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.4 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not estimated to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and the wood stove vent up a chimney and can be susceptible to back drafting. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit, I would suggest that a dedicated outside combustion air pipe be directly connected to your Blaze King wood stove,

During my assessment of your home, there were smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Fort Smith

The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:
Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.25 per kWh
Oil  - $1.17 per litre 
Propane - $0.87 per litre
Wood - $242 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $8.49 per bag, $288.46 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
Phone: (867) 920-3333
Fax: (867) 873-0303
E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and ation costs, and climate change!ortation costs, and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
